sec.p:
Specifically, for a time series segment {xt} of length N, we constructed delay embeddings of dimension m and delay τ:
(1)
vt=(xt,xt+τ,…,xt+(m−1)τ),t=1,…,N−(m−1)τ. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{v}_{t}=\left(x_{t}, x_{t+\tau}, \ldots, x_{t+(m-1) \tau}\right), \quad t=1, \ldots, N-(m-1) \tau . \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Each embedding vector vt was mapped to one of m! possible ordinal patterns by recording the rank ordering of its components. The same procedure was applied to the time-reversed signal {xN+1−t}, yielding a backward ordinal pattern distribution. The symmetrized Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence between the forward and backward distributions served as our EPR estimator:
(2)
Dsym=DKL(Pfwd‖Pbwd)+DKL(Pbwd‖Pfwd), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} D_{\mathrm{sym}}=D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(P_{\mathrm{fwd}} \| P_{\mathrm{bwd}}\right)+D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(P_{\mathrm{bwd}} \| P_{\mathrm{fwd}}\right), \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Pfwd and Pbwd denote the normalized ordinal pattern frequency distributions of the forward and reversed signal, respectively, and where the KL divergence between two discrete distributions P and Q over the m! ordinal patterns πi is:
(3)
DKL(P‖Q)=∑i=1m!P(πi)log⁡P(πi)Q(πi). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q)=\sum_{i=1}^{m!} P\left(\pi_{i}\right) \log \frac{P\left(\pi_{i}\right)}{Q\left(\pi_{i}\right)} . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Additive Laplace smoothing (ε = 10−6 per bin) was applied before normalization to avoid undefined logarithms.

Model A (baseline + amplitude only) included only prestimulation EPR and the window-matched fluorescence change as predictors:
(5)
Dsymijwindow=β0+β1Dsymijbase+β2ΔGCaMPz,ij+uj+εij, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle {D_\mathrm{sym}}_{ij}^{\mathrm{window }}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1} D_{\mathrm{sym }_{i j}}^{\mathrm{base }}+\beta_{2} \Delta \mathrm{GCaMP}_{z, i j}+u_{j}+\varepsilon_{i j},$$\end{document}
where ΔGCaMPz,i j is the standardized change in mean calcium amplitude for repeated measure i of animal j. Model B (shared dose) added linear and quadratic intensity terms that were common to both on-and off-target conditions:
(6)
Dsymijwindow=β0+β1Dsymijbase+β2ΔGCaMPz,ij+β3Ic+β4Ic2+uj+εij. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle {D_\mathrm{sym}}_{i j}^{\mathrm{window}}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1} D_{\mathrm{sym}_{i j}}^{\mathrm{base}}+\beta_{2} \Delta \mathrm{GCaMP}_{z, i j}+\beta_{3} I_{c}+\beta_{4} I_{c}^{2}+u_{j}+\varepsilon_{i j}.$$\end{document}
Model C (condition-specific dose) added an on-target fixed effect and further allowed the dose dependence to differ between on-target and off-target stimulation:
(7)
Dsymijwindow=β0+β1Dsymijbase+β2ΔGCaMPz,ij+β3Ic+β4Ic2+β5OnTargetij+β6OnTargetijIc+β7OnTargetijIc2+uj+εij. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} {D_\mathrm{sym}}_{i j}^{\text {window}}=& \beta_{0}+\beta_{1} D_{\mathrm{sym }_{i j}}^{\mathrm{base}}+\beta_{2} \Delta \mathrm{GCaMP}_{z, i j}+\beta_{3} I_{c}+\beta_{4} I_{c}^{2} \\ & +\beta_{5} \mathrm{OnTarget}_{i j}+\beta_{6} \mathrm{OnTarget}_{i j} I_{c}+\beta_{7} \mathrm{OnTarget }_{i j} I_{c}^{2}+u_{j}+\varepsilon_{i j} . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
We employed likelihood-ratio (LR) tests to assess significant increases in explained variance: comparing Model B to Model A tests whether acoustic dose explains any EPR variance beyond baseline state and calcium amplitude change. Comparing Model C to Model B tests whether the dose–EPR relationship differs between on-target and off-target stimulation. Finally, to ask whether dose shapes EPR within the on-target condition alone, we refit Models A and B on the on-target subset only and compared them with a third LR test.

For each trial, we computed ordinal EPR and mean GCaMP amplitude separately for the baseline, stimulation, and recovery windows. Trial-level change scores were defined as ΔDsym = Dsymwindow − Dsymbaseline and ΔGCaMP = x¯\begin{document}$\bar{x}$\end{document}
window − x¯\begin{document}$\bar{x}$\end{document}
baseline, where x¯\begin{document}$\bar{x}$\end{document} denotes the mean calcium amplitude within the specified window.

To test whether baseline state modulates the response to tFUS, we pooled trials across acoustic intensities and discretised baseline EPR into terciles (Low, Mid, High). For the EPR outcome measure, we fit a hierarchy of mixed-effects models according to:
(8)
Null:Dsymijwindow=μ+βonOnTargetij+αIij+uj+ϵij, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text{Null:} \quad {D_\mathrm{sym}}_{ij}^{\mathrm{window}} = \mu + \beta_{\mathrm{on}} \mathrm{OnTarget}_{ij} + \alpha_{I_{ij}} + u_j + \epsilon_{ij},$$\end{document}

For GCaMP, the same three-model hierarchy was applied with calcium amplitude as the out-come and baseline GCaMP added as a covariate to adjust for prestimulation fluorescence:
(11)
Null:x‾ijwindow=μ+βblx‾ijbaseline+βonOnTargetij+αIij+uj+ϵij, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text{Null:} \quad \bar{x}_{ij}^{\mathrm{window}} = \mu + \beta_{\mathrm{bl}} \bar{x}_{ij}^{\mathrm{baseline}} + \beta_{\mathrm{on}} \mathrm{OnTarget}_{ij} + \alpha_{I_{ij}} + u_j + \epsilon_{ij},$$\end{document}
